    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 220\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 3146, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the social services law, in relation to directing\n      the office of children and family services to  establish  workload\n      standards for child protective services workers\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  require  the Office of Children and Family Services\n(OCFS) to set caseload standards that would limit the caseloads of  full\ntime child protective service (CPS) workers.\n \n  I vetoed an identical bill last year because imposing a caseload stan-\ndard  without  flexibility may result in CPS investigations being rushed\nor closed prematurely, and a one-size-fits-all approach to caseloads may\nnot be suitable for all agencies. (Veto No. 301 of 2016).  I also  noted\nthat any legislation seeking to mandate a caseload standard must include\nadequate  funding.  Accordingly, I directed OCFS to conduct a roundtable\non this topic with the bill's sponsors, county officials, and interested\nstakeholders.\n \n  Based on OCFS's findings, and from additional engagement  with  stake-\nholders,  I  still  have significant concerns about mandating a caseload\nstandard for CPS workers. First, the complexity  and  work  required  on\nindividual  CPS  investigations  differs  greatly  based on a variety of\nfactors, including the severity of the allegations, the number of  chil-\ndren  in  the household, whether removal or family court intervention is\nsought, the involvement of law  enforcement,  and  the  availability  of\nevidence.  A  one-size-fits-all  caseload  standard fails to account for\nthese factors and does not afford social services districts  the  flexi-\nbility  to provide for effective investigation of alleged child abuse or\nmaltreatment. Second, it may place children at  risk,  as  it  may  lead\nagencies  to  prematurely  close  investigations  in order to keep their\ncaseloads within an arbitrary  caseload  standard.  Finally,  this  bill\nwould  impose  unbudgeted costs on taxpayers, as there is no established\nfunding source to assist local governments that would need to hire addi-\ntional workers.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
